    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Currie-Gymnasium/Teaching-Assistant-EDKP-396-KPE-F26_JR0000076340": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant EDKP 396 KPE F26",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Kinesiology and Physical Education Course Title: Adapted Physical Activity Course Code: EDKP 396 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 450 This posting is for two to five unionized contractual appointments. A Teaching Assistant is a Graduate Student employed to assist the Course Supervisor as a Teaching Assistant in a classroom environment. Teaching Assistant Positions are governed by the Collective Agreement between McGill University and the Association of Graduate Students Employed at McGill (AGSEM). Supplemental Posting for 1 Position @ 50 hours total. Application period 2 days (Sept. 14 to 16, 2026 ending at mid-night). Position Summary: This requisition is for up to five positions, at 45 0 hours total (min. 90 hours each) for the term, in the Department Kinesiology and Physical Education . Required Duties : - Assist with practicum which involves overseeing undergraduates teach children, adolescents and adults with a disability in a physical activity setting. - Supervises designated undergraduates and meet with the students during the semester to discuss their program planning. - Assists with the development of class assignments - Grades or marks class assignments and-or at-home exams and-or assignments - Engage periodically in virtual contact time with students about course-related questions - Will be asked to periodically attend lectures upon the professor's request Qualifications: - Undergraduate degree in PE/Kin - Experience working in physical activity settings with individuals who have a disability. Language Requirements: Fluency in French is an asset Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $37.34 Position Start Date: 2026-08-23 Position End Date: 2026-12-26 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-16 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Redpath-Library/Research-Data-Management-Specialist_JR0000080513": {
      "title": "Research Data Management Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Academic Associate or Senior academic Associate (Unranked Contract Academic Staff) Duration: three years, with possibility of renewal Salary minimum: salary commensurate with experience The Research Data Management (RDM) Specialist supports the development and delivery of research data management services within the University. As a member of the McGill Libraries’ Research & Scholarship unit, the incumbent contributes to the Libraries' broader mission of advancing research, teaching, learning, scholarly communication, and open knowledge initiatives. Working collaboratively across the Libraries, the position serves as an integral member of the Digital Research Services (DRS) team, a collaborative initiative of the Office of the Vice-Principal (Research and Innovation), the Libraries, and Information Technology, and contributes to initiatives that advance RDM services, digital scholarship, open science, and emerging research methodologies. THE McGILL LIBRARIES Located in Montréal, one of the world’s great multicultural and multilingual cities, McGill University is internationally recognized for its excellence as a leading institution of higher education and research. For nearly 200 years, through the work of dedicated people, McGill has been breaking ground in diverse fields and contributing solutions to some of the world’s most significant issues. McGill is also the most internationally diverse research-intensive university in Canada, with over 32% of its students coming to McGill from more than 150 countries. The University systematically appears on the annual list of the best employers in Montreal.  The McGill University Libraries contribute to society by advancing teaching, learning, and research in a supportive and welcoming environment. We anticipate and respond to the needs of users by providing outstanding collections, excellent services, and spaces that inspire. We work in collaboration with global scholarly and library communities to enhance and facilitate access to knowledge. The McGill Libraries has the largest collection in Quebec and is one of Canada’s largest academic libraries with over six million monograph titles and 150,000 e-journals and databases. The Libraries are a member of the Canadian Association of Research Libraries (CARL), the Association of Research Libraries (ARL), the Partenariat des bibliothèques universitaires du Québec (PBUQ), the Center for Research Libraries (CRL), and the HathiTrust Digital Library. INFORMATION FOR PROSPECTIVE STAFF Information about the  University  and the McGill  Libraries  can be found on the University’s web site. The McGill Libraries have developed  Strategic Priorities for 2025-2030  to guide its actions. Information regarding the employment of contract academic staff can be found in the  Regulations relating to the employment of contract academic staff . DUTY STATEMENT Primary Purpose of Position To support the development and delivery of research data management services within the University. Duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Serve as an integral member of the Digital Research Services (DRS) team within the Office of Research and Innovation. Partner with liaison librarians and other library colleagues to embed research data management services within subject-based and discipline-specific library programs. Develop and enhance library-based services that support research impact, open scholarship, and data stewardship. Provide expert consultation and support to faculty, students, and staff on data management planning, management, use, curation, deposit and sharing.  Design and deliver instructional programs, and learning materials on RDM topics. Conduct outreach to faculty, graduate students, research staff, and other stakeholders regarding RDM services. Collaborate with library colleagues and campus partners to advance RDM, digital scholarship, scholarly communications, open science, and other research support initiatives. Build and maintain partnerships with academic departments, research centres, information technology, and external organizations (e.g., the Digital Research Alliance of Canada) to enhance RDM support services. Serve on various library, faculty, and university committees and represent the Libraries on external committees (e.g., PBUQ comité sur la gestion des données de recherche). Monitor developments in RDM services and emerging technologies to inform service development and strategic planning. Advance the equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility as well as reconciliation with Indigenous Peoples through the McGill University Libraries' services and of the Libraries as a workplace. The duties listed above are representative and not exhaustive; responsibilities may evolve in response to institutional and library needs. Reporting relationship This position reports to the Associate Dean, Research and Scholarship.   SELECTION CRITERIA Required: Ph.D. in a research data-focused discipline.  Experience providing research support, library services, scholarly communications, digital scholarship, or research data management services in an academic library, research, or equivalent environment. Demonstrated understanding of the academic research lifecycle and the role of academic libraries in supporting research, teaching, learning, scholarly communication, open scholarship, open science, and research data management. Demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships with researchers, librarians, academic departments, and campus partners. Experience designing and delivering instruction, workshops, consultations, and educational resources in a library or research support environment. Strong written and oral communication skills. Fluency in oral and written English is required. McGill University is an English-language university where day-to-day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing.  Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility of services or work environment. Desired: Experience with data stewardship and RDM concepts, including the development and review of data management plans and the use of data repositories, such as Borealis.  Knowledge of open science, FAIR data principles, and awareness of the CARE Principles for Indigenous Data Governance is an asset. Ability to work flexibly in a changing environment. Bilingualism (English/French) is an asset. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   The McGill Libraries are committed to advancing open, equitable, and sustainable research practices and welcomes candidates who will contribute to these priorities. APPLICATION PROCEDURE Applications should address the above selection criteria, be accompanied by: a letter of interest,  a curriculum vitae,  the names and addresses of three referees.   *** Applicants are encouraged to indicate any accommodation needs related to job duties. We anticipate conducting in-person interviews between November 9 and 20, 2026. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000080353": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Prof. Natasha Chang's Lab - The Department of Biochemistry Supervisor: Prof. Natasha Chang Position Summary: The laboratory of Prof. Natasha Chang in the Department of Biochemistry at McGill University is seeking a highly motivated Research Assistant to support translational research in RNA therapeutics, skeletal muscle biology, and regenerative medicine. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to contribute to collaborative research programs focused on developing novel therapeutic approaches for muscle diseases. The successful candidate will: Participate in the design and execution of experiments supporting the laboratory's research programs in RNA therapeutics, skeletal muscle biology, and regenerative medicine. Perform molecular and cellular biology techniques, including cell culture, nucleic acid analysis, protein analysis, and functional assays. Collect, analyze, interpret, and present experimental data. Optimize, adapt, and implement experimental protocols to advance the laboratory's research objectives. Maintain accurate experimental records and prepare research reports, presentations, and manuscripts. Assist with project planning, milestone tracking, and collaborations with research partners. Assist in the preparation of grant applications, progress reports, and knowledge dissemination activities. Provide technical guidance and training to students and junior laboratory personnel as required. Support general laboratory operations and administrative activities, including inventory management, purchasing, and budget tracking.  Educational Qualifications: Master's degree in Molecular Biology, Cell Biology, Biochemistry, Pharmacology, Physiology, or a related discipline. Minimum of 3 to 5 years of relevant laboratory research experience in academia or industry. Demonstrated expertise in RNA biology, nucleic acid therapeutics, skeletal muscle biology, or related fields. Experience in designing, troubleshooting, and executing research experiments. Additional Skills & Qualifications: Experience in skeletal muscle biology, muscle stem cell biology, RNA therapeutics, or nucleic acid delivery is strongly preferred. Hands-on experience with primary mammalian cell culture. Proficiency in molecular biology techniques, including siRNA transfection/delivery, RT-qPCR, digital PCR, Western blotting, and Simple Western. Experience with flow cytometry and/or cell sorting is an asset. Experience handling laboratory mice and performing routine procedures, including injections and tissue collection. Strong scientific writing, data analysis, and presentation skills. Excellent organizational skills, attention to detail, and record keeping. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative research environment. Interest in translational research and industry-academic collaborations.  Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: McIntyre Medical Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-28 Position End Date: 2027-09-27 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-18 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
