    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Invigilator---Department-of-Psychology_JR0000080296": {
      "title": "Invigilator - Department of Psychology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Psychology Exam Period: Psyc 211 - Introduction to Behavioral Neuroscience – September 28th – 15:30 – 18:00  Psyc 408 - Principles and Applications of Psychotherapy – September 29th – 10:45 – 13:45  Psyc 311 - Human Cognition and the Brain – October 1st – 15:30 – 18:00  Psyc 444 - Sleep Mechanisms and Behaviors – October 5th – 14:00 – 16:30  Psyc 337 – Introduction to Psychopathology – October 7th, 16:00 – 18:30  Psyc 341 - The Psychology of Bilingualism – October 8th – 14:00 – 16:30  Psyc 406 – Psychological Tests - October 8th – 15:30 – 18:00  Psyc 332 – Introduction to Personality – October 8th – 17:00 – 19:30  Psyc 100 – Introduction to Psychology - October 20th – 9:30 – 12:00  Psyc 305 – Statistics for Experimental Design - October 26th – 9:30 – 12:00  Psyc 311 - Human Cognition and the Brain – November 3rd – 15:30 – 18:00  Psyc 100 – Introduction to Psychology – November 3rd – 9:30 – 12:00  Psyc 215 - Social Psychology – November 4th – 14:00 – 16:30  Psyc 211 - Introduction to Behavioral Neuroscience – November 9th - 15:30 – 18:00  Psyc 408 - Principles and Applications of Psychotherapy – November 10th – 10:45 – 13:45  Psyc 337 – Introduction to Psychopathology – November 11th, 16:00 – 18:30  Psyc 444 - Sleep Mechanisms and Behaviors – November 16th – 14:00 – 16:30  Psyc 444 - Sleep Mechanisms and Behaviors – December 2nd – 14:00 – 16:30  Invigilators will need to come to 2001 McGill College 30 minutes before the exam and they will need to be available 30 minutes after the exam is over.   The hours listed include setup and takedown. Position Summary: Responsible for helping with the set of the examination room according to specific requirements. Helps administer the delivery of the exams and documentation. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Alerts professor when students have a question or a concern, if arises. At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensure delivery of the exams.     Qualifications: Must be client-focused and service oriented.  Punctual. Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately. Demonstrates ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems. Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team. Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams. English, spoken and read. French an asset.   An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their Supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-19 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Human-Resources-Advisor_JR0000080346-1": {
      "title": "Human Resources Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary:   Reporting to the Associate Director, Administration and Finance, the Human Resources (HR) Advisor provides a full range of HR services to Enrollment Services within the Office of the Provost and Executive Vice-President (Academic).  The incumbent acts as a trusted advisor to managers and employees, providing professional guidance and support on a comprehensive range of HR matters, aligning with Enrolment Services' mission and objectives  The HR Advisor serves as a knowledgeable and reliable point of contact, offering guidance on matters of labour and employee relations, training, benefits performance management, workforce planning, compensation, organizational effectiveness, talent development, and employee engagement initiatives and other core areas of HR, while actively contributing to the development and implementation of  personnel policies and procedures and new initiatives that align with the unit's strategic direction.  Working collaboratively with stakeholders across the University, the incumbent promotes a positive, inclusive, and respectful workplace culture and supports the achievement of departmental and institutional goals.    Primary Responsibilities:   Advise and guide managers and employees on the interpretation and application of human resources policies, procedures, collective agreements, and employment legislation, ensuring consistent and effective HR practices across the unit.  Advise and support managers on employee and labour relations matters, including performance and attendance management, workplace accommodations, disciplinary matters, conflict resolution, investigations, grievance administration, and other complex employee relations issues. Liaise with Labour Relations, as appropriate, on matters involving collective agreements, disciplinary action, grievances, and arbitration.  Partner with managers and HR colleagues to support workforce planning, recruitment and staffing activities, including identifying workforce needs, developing job postings, participating in candidate assessment and interviews, and supporting hiring recommendations. Leverage workforce data and trends to anticipate staffing requirements and inform workforce planning.  Partner with managers to develop and implement initiatives that support employee engagement, retention, professional development, succession planning, and organizational effectiveness. Identify learning and development needs and coordinate or facilitate appropriate training and development opportunities.  Conduct job assessments and audits and contribute to organizational reviews, position classification, compensation recommendations, job abolitions, and organizational structure changes. Support the implementation of organizational changes and continuous improvement initiatives, working collaboratively with managers, HR colleagues, and stakeholders to enhance service delivery, operational effectiveness, and the employee experience.  Provide advice and support related to disability management, short- and long-term disability, parental and other leaves, attendance management, workplace accommodations, and leave entitlements, ensuring appropriate application of policies, legislation, and collective agreement provisions.  Use HR systems, including Workday and other HR tools, to manage HR requests, maintain accurate employee information, generate reports, and analyze workforce data. Identify trends and provide insights and recommendations to support evidence-based decision-making and proactive people management.  Contribute to the development, maintenance, and communication of HR policies and practices in accordance with current legislation, collective agreements, and organizational requirements, and proactively communicate relevant changes to managers and employees.  Collaborate with HR colleagues, Talent Acquisition, Labour Relations, and stakeholders across administrative units to ensure integrated, consistent, and effective HR service delivery.  Participate in HR projects, committees, and special initiatives in support of departmental and institutional priorities, bringing HR expertise to organizational and strategic initiatives.  Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities:    Undergrad degree in Administration, Human Resources, or a related field  CHRA certification a strong asset.   Proven experience in working in a complex unionized environment and in-depth understanding of labor legislation.   Client-focused with demonstrated ability to work autonomously and think critically.   Demonstrated ability to analyze information, solve problems, and provide recommendations and solutions.  Demonstrated ability to prioritize tasks and meet deadlines while managing multiple, often conflicting, demands.    Strong interpersonal skills and ability to handle challenging situations and difficult conversations.    Experience in addressing emotionally charged interactions with empathy, diplomacy, calm demeanour, and sound judgment.    Strong attention to detail with the ability to maintain confidential personnel files.   Proficient with Microsoft suite (Excel, Word, and PowerPoint and Outlook).  Experience with HR Information Systems like Workday is an asset.   Demonstrated effective verbal and written communication skills in English and French when working with staff at all levels.   As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-HRM2D - Human Resources Generalist - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, Administration and Finance Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Ferrier-Building/Building-Operator_JR0000078182": {
      "title": "Building Operator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Facilities Management and Ancillary Services - HVAC Operations Position Summary: Under the supervision of the HVAC Supervisor, Zone East – the Building Operator is responsible for the operation and maintenance of heating, refrigeration and other building engineering equipment assigned under his care. Qualifications: Must possess valid certificates of Fourth Class Stationary Engineering along with a class “B” (Refrigeration Equipment Operations). English and French functional spoken and read. English or French functional written. Additional Details: Unit: HVAC Premium: Certification (0.60$/hour) Status: Full Time Hours of Work: 38.75 hours/week, day shift from 7:30 am to 3:45 pm; the incumbent will be required to serve on a call-in list. A meal time period of thirty (30) minutes is unpaid. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days (if eligible) Two (2) floating holidays (if eligible) Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day (if eligible) Paid time off over the December holiday period (if eligible) Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 2 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Hourly Salary: $35.48 Hours per Week: 38.75 (Full time) Location: Ferrier Building Supervisor: Supervisor Building Operations Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-22 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Gelber-Law-Library/Senior-Library-Clerk---Circulation-Services_JR0000079772": {
      "title": "Senior Library Clerk - Circulation Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Nahum Gelber Law Library (McGill University Libraries) Schedule: Monday to Friday, 9 am to 5 pm. Work done 100% on-site Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, provides frontline service and operational support in the Nahum Gelber Law Library. Coordinates day-to-day activities within assigned service areas, assists with the training and guidance of staff and student employees, and contributes to the delivery of high-quality user services. Supports access to library collections through circulation, resource sharing, reserves management, collection maintenance, and the provision of information regarding library services, resources, and procedures. Maintains records, equipment, and supplies related to library operations. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Provides circulation services, including the lending, return, renewal, recall, and retrieval of library materials, and issues borrowing privileges in accordance with library policies. Assists in the coordination of daily service desk operations by organizing workflows, providing guidance to staff and student employees, monitoring service delivery, and helping to resolve operational issues. Processes payments and maintains related records, and supports activities related to fines, overdue materials, and user notifications. Supports interlibrary loan and document delivery services by receiving requests, verifying availability of materials, retrieving eligible items, and coordinating requests with partner institutions. Maintains records, reports, invoices, and statistical information related to interlibrary loans, overdue materials, and other circulation activities. Manages course reserve materials by processing, updating, organizing, and monitoring access to high-demand resources. Assists users by providing information about library services, policies, spaces, and collections, and communicates with users regarding requests, recalls, holds, and other circulation matters. Provides basic research and reference assistance by helping users navigate the library catalogue, discovery tools, databases, and other information resources, and refers complex inquiries to librarians when appropriate. Supports the processing and physical maintenance of library materials by receiving and preparing items, applying barcodes and security devices, preparing materials for binding, and carrying out routine repairs when appropriate. Maintains accurate records and files, updates databases, prepares reports, and compiles statistical information related to library operations. Contributes to the continuous improvement of service delivery by participating in the development and refinement of workflows, procedures, and operational practices. Opens and closes service areas as required, monitors the condition of public and staff spaces, maintains equipment and supplies, assists users with equipment, and arranges servicing when necessary. Coordinates bookings and reservations for study spaces, meeting rooms, and other library resources in accordance with established procedures. Supports collection maintenance activities by retrieving, sorting, shelving, and organizing materials; conducting shelf-reading and inventory projects; and participating in collection shifts and space management initiatives. Uses a variety of technologies and office equipment, including library management systems, computers, scanners, photocopiers, and other specialized tools required to support library services. Performs other related duties consistent with the responsibilities of the position. The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: DEC in Information and Library Technologies or equivalent experience. Bachelor's degree is an asset. Experience working in a client service focused environment with a proven ability to deal with people at all levels. Organizational, customer service and teamwork skills, problem solving ability, proven ability to work with automated systems, and a general understanding of a library environment. Physical requirements include moving and lifting weights of up to 20 kgs., handling dusty and other various types of library materials, shelving and shifting books, bending, walking (includes walking between buildings), working while standing. Demonstrated ability to work in a computerized environment. English, spoken and written. French, spoken and written. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties require English communication both verbally and in writing. Experience in a law library or legal knowledge (preferred) As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Secretarial Studies 3 Years Related Experience / DEP - Office Systems Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level F) $29.42 - $36.46 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Librarian Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
