    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Rhetoric-and-Communications-of-Public-Policy---PPOL-641_JR0000080367-1": {
      "title": "Rhetoric and Communications of Public Policy - PPOL 641",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Max Bell School of Public Policy Fall Term 2026 Course Policy Complexity Seminar in MPP PPOL 641 Rhetoric and Communications of Public Policy (2 credits) Teaching Scheudule Mon-Tue-Wed-Thu-Fri Between 9am - 12 pm and 1pm-4pm Total 30 hours per week Course Start and End Dates October 19 to 23, 2026 Location 680 Sherbrooke St. West, #650 Salary $8,830   Teaching Qualification Requirements Bachelor, M.A. and/or Ph.D. degree in relevant field Teaching experience at University level is preferred Extensive knowledge of policy transparency and policy transparency challenges Experience Strong background in public policy, policy analysis, politics, analytics, or economics is required Minimum of five years of applied/industry-related experience complemented by academic research in relevant field is essential Proven experience in the design, analysis and implementation of policies within government or industry settings, with a clear understanding of complexities inherent in the policymaking process Demonstrable experience in persuasive communication - both written and verbal - and its influence on the policymaking process is preferred Course Description Master in Public Policy (MPP) Complexity Seminars are two-credit intensive courses delivered over one week (six hours per day) during the Fall, Winter, and/or Summer terms. Each seminar focuses on a specific dimension of complexity within the policymaking process. These courses are designed to explore a particular type of complexity as it manifests across a range of policy issues, including but not limited to transparency challenges in public finance, central banking, trade, income inequality, climate change, and access to information legislation. Practitioners leading these seminars should have expertise that closely aligns with the seminar’s thematic focus. Hiring Unit: Max Bell School of Public Policy Course Title: Rhetoric and Communications of Public Policy Subject Code: PPOL 641 Complexity Seminar 1 Location: 680 Sherbrooke St. Ouest #650 Schedule: M-T-W-T-F Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Trottier-Building/Course-Lecturer---Charg-de-cours-AERO-520_JR0000080371": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer / Chargé de cours AERO 520",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). COURSE POSTING, COURSE LECTURER/INSTRUCTOR AFFICHAGE DE COURS, CHARGÉE DE COURS/INSTRUCTEUR(TRICE) Hiring Unit: McGill Institute for Aerospace Engineering Course Title: Introduction to Aerospace Design Subject Code: AERO 520 Term: Winter 2027 Location: McGill Downtown Campus/McGill Centre-Ville Schedule: Tuesday 5:35 p.m. to 8:25 p.m. Preparation and course lectures: 9 out of 13 lectures, from January 5 to April 14, 2026. The incumbent will provide lectures for the 3-credit course AERO 520 on aerospace-design related topics, including sizing of aircrafts; configuration design; design of fuselage, wing, and empennage; stability, control, and performance; component weight estimation; and aerodynamics and thrust characteristics. The incumbent will guide students in the use of Advanced Aircraft Analysis (AAA) or equivalent software in relation to these topics. The software will provide the basis for the design projects, undertaken by teams of students, that will constitute the majority of the assigned work in the course. In addition, the incumbent will offer guidance and feedback on the design projects. Salary: The salary will be pro-rated to 9/13 of the standard MCLIU Course Lecturer salary ($11,824) and will equal $8,198. Unité d’embauche : Institut de génie aérospatiale de l’Université McGill Titre du cours : Introduction à la conception aérospatiale Code du cours : AERO 520 Semestre : Hiver 2027 Lieu : Campus McGill Downtown / McGill Centre-Ville Horaire : Mardi, de 17 h 35 à 20 h 25 Préparation et cours magistraux : 9 cours sur 13, du 5 janvier au 14 avril 2026. Le titulaire donnera des cours magistraux pour le cours AERO 520 (3 crédits) sur des sujets liés à la conception aérospatiale, notamment le dimensionnement des aéronefs; la conception de la configuration; la conception du fuselage, des ailes et de l’empennage; la stabilité, le contrôle et la performance; l’estimation du poids des composants; ainsi que l’aérodynamique et les caractéristiques de poussée. Le titulaire guidera les étudiants dans l’utilisation du logiciel Advanced Aircraft Analysis (AAA) ou d’un logiciel équivalent en lien avec ces sujets. Ce logiciel servira de base aux projets de conception, réalisés par des équipes d’étudiants, qui constitueront la majeure partie du travail demandé dans le cadre du cours. De plus, le titulaire offrira des conseils et des commentaires sur les projets de conception. Salaire : Le salaire sera calculé au prorata à raison de 9/13 du salaire standard d’un chargé de cours au MCLIU (11 824 $) et s’élèvera à 8 198 $. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Minimum of Master’s degree in Engineering Ideally member of Order of Engineers, though not required. Extensive knowledge of Advanced Aircraft Analysis (AAA) or equivalent software. Experience with supervising and guiding students through design projects. Extensive experience with Aerospace Engineering Design and Engineering Team Management. Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English. Strong problem-solving skills and flexible approach to teaching. Exigences relatives aux qualifications d’enseignement : Au minimum, une maîtrise en génie. Idéalement, être membre de l’Ordre des ingénieurs, bien que cela ne soit pas obligatoire. Connaissance approfondie du logiciel Advanced Aircraft Analysis (AAA) ou d’un logiciel équivalent. Expérience dans la supervision et l’encadrement d’étudiants dans le cadre de projets de conception. Vaste expérience en conception en génie aérospatial et en gestion d’équipes d’ingénieurs. Maîtrise avérée de l’anglais parlé et écrit. Solides compétences en résolution de problèmes et approche flexible de l’enseignement. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Avant de postuler, veuillez noter que pour travailler à l’Université McGill, vous devez à la fois être autorisé à travailler au Canada et être disposé à travailler au Québec, sur le campus où se trouve le poste. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L'Université McGill est une université anglophone où la plupart des activités d'enseignement et de recherche se déroulent en anglais, ce qui exige de pouvoir communiquer en anglais tant à l'oral qu'à l'écrit. Hiring Unit: McGill Institute for Aerospace Engineering Course Title: Introduction to Aerospace Design Subject Code: AERO 520 1 Location: Trottier 0070 Schedule: Mondays 5:35-8:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Montreal-Neuro/Lab-Helper-B_JR0000080342": {
      "title": "Lab Helper B",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: The Neuro Position Summary: Working in the laboratory of Dr Bernard Brais, the Lab Helper B will assist a lab associate and research fellow with simple repetitive experiments such as genomic DNA extraction, designing primers for DNA amplification, PCR amplification, sequencing, analysing the results, RNA extraction, single nuclei extraction, cell culture. The Lab Helper B will learn different techniques such as DNA or RNA extraction from different sources of tissue require different protocols, working with DNA and RNA requires different approaches of working depending on the outcomes. This position is covered by AMUSE Collective Agreement. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $19.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 28 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-09-21 Position End Date: 2027-09-20 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-16 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
