    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Student-Recruitment-Manager--Quebec-and-Virtual-_JR0000080661-1": {
      "title": "Student Recruitment Manager (Quebec and Virtual)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Student Recruitment Manager (Quebec and Virtual) Position Summary :  Reporting to the Associate Director, Recruitment and Communications, this role will support the coordination, implementation, and evaluation of recruitment and yield strategies developed for the Quebec market and virtual recruitment programming. The incumbent will provide quality advice and service on a diverse range of admissions matters to applicants, students, staff, the public, and senior management.   Primary Responsibilities: Contributes to and participates in the implementation of initiatives that support the strategic direction of the unit and university enrolment priorities for undergraduate admissions by keeping abreast of best practices, new developments, technology, and trends in the fields of recruitment and enrolment. Collaborates with other Managers and Supervisors on the Recruitment and Communications team to support the Associate Director with the overall management of day-to-day functions of the department. Provides admissions, recruitment, scholarships, and student services information in support of the University's mission of academic excellence. , Develops, manages and delivers virtual programming for domestic and international prospective students, applicants, and admitted students creating opportunities for them to engage with various units at the university, including but not limited to professors, advisors, and student services staff. Plans, evaluates, reports, and makes recommendations on new initiatives or improvements to current virtual recruitment programming in alignment with enrolment priorities and in collaboration with the Recruitment and Communications Managers and Welcome Centre Supervisor. Proposes and delivers a variety of virtual recruitment activities, including hosting webinars through Zoom, Slate, Webex, as well as drop-in sessions and fairs hosted by third parties, etc. Contributes to the development and implementation of the Quebec recruitment and yield strategy through planning, execution, monitoring, and continuous improvement. Ensures follow-up on issues raised during and after visits/events for all Quebec applicant pools. Builds and maintains relationships with recruitment and admissions personnel working in faculties to explore opportunities for collaboration for Quebec recruitment and yield and ensure alignment of McGill’s undergraduate enrolment efforts in Quebec. Analyzes, develops, and recommends to the Associate Director an annual communications plan for virtual recruitment activities and Quebec recruitment, and manages, in consultation with the Recruitment and Communications Managers, a virtual recruitment calendar that takes into consideration the unit’s global recruitment and yield strategy and the needs of each region (domestic and international). Uses the current recruiting software to plan, schedule, and track activities, budgets and KPIs. Develops appropriate measurement criteria to evaluate the effectiveness of virtual recruitment programming and Quebec recruitment events and activities, which includes, but is not limited to providing data and other information required to support the planning, implementation and measurement (KPIs) of recruitment strategies and activities. Reports on market activity through targeted observations, research, professional networks, and internal and external reports. Participates in budget planning and recommends resource allocation for Quebec and virtual recruitment. Participates and supports biannual recruitment trainings and takes part in professional development activities to remain well informed of university practices and programs, and ensures strong delivery of information, and support of prospective students and their support networks. Responsible for the hire and supervision of direct reports, workload distribution, continual evaluation, including training and development. Manages and coaches a team of unionized staff members. Acts as resource person in handling conflicts and difficult situations. Empowers staff by mentoring and coaching. Ensures that direct reports are well informed of University practices and programs to ensure strong delivery of information and in support of our prospective students and their support networks by the recruitment team. Builds and maintains relationships with Faculties and other internal McGill units to explore opportunities for collaboration to support recruitment in accordance with established Enrolment Services goals and objectives and the University's overall mission. Represents McGill, attends in-person, virtual and on-campus events as well as CEGEP and high school visits in Quebec, prepares and delivers presentations and documents and ensures meetings and follow up with students (as needed), including regarding any opportunities or issues raised during and after visits Represents the Recruitment team on working groups, project teams and committees, as required. May be called upon to support the Welcome Centre, by meeting with prospective student visitors and answering individual questions.   Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Supervisory experience a requirement. Experience advising potential students on admissions criteria. Knowledge of the student life cycle. Experience in student recruitment in Quebec. McGill undergraduate experience an asset. Ability to support staff in their daily recruitment and administrative operations. Proven experience in data analysis: ability to review statistics and reports, condense, recap, and evaluate data to propose and plan effective recruitment activities. Proven organizational skills, ability to prioritize, and multi-task accurately with attention to detail. Experience working in a PC environment using databases, presentation software, web tools, CRMs, and Microsoft Teams. Excellent interpersonal, communication, public speaking, and writing skills. Proven experience organizing events and fairs locally and at out-of-town venues. Experience in the creation of dynamic presentations, an asset. Proven ability to work autonomously and as a member of a team. Valid Quebec Driver’s license and valid Passport. Availability to travel short and long distances extensively and using various modes of transportation. Ability to work flexible hours, including evenings, and weekends. English and French written and spoken. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing.   As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2A - Student Enrolment - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, Recruitment and Communications Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-04 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Course-Lecturer---CANC-621D2_JR0000080554": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - CANC 621D2",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The PhD in Interdisciplinary Cancer Sciences Program (ICSP) of the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, McGill University, is seeking applications for a Course Lecturer for the 2026-2027 academic year to teach the CANC 621 D2 Seminars in Cancer Sciences course, an interdisciplinary, PhD level course. This posting is specifically for the Pillar 4 (Psychosocial Oncology) Course Coordinator role. The course is co-coordinated by four Course Coordinators, one representing each ICSP pillar. The successful candidate will serve as the Pillar 4 (Psychosocial Oncology) Course Coordinator, working collaboratively with the other three coordinators to deliver a cohesive and interdisciplinary learning experience. This appointment is made in accordance with the McGill University Course Lecturer Agreement and is a non - tenure - track, contractually defined teaching appointment. Course Description CANC 621 D1/D2 – Seminars in Cancer Sciences is a multi-term course offered over the Fall and Winter terms. The course exposes ICSP PhD students to a broad range of cancer research disciplines through attendance at seminars by leading cancer researchers in different fields as well as in-class journal clubs and discussion. The course will focus on the following cancer research disciplines: cancer biology, cancer therapeutics and care, cancer epidemiology and global cancer control, and psychosocial oncology. By the end of this course, students will have gained a better understanding of the types of cancer research conducted within and outside of students' area of expertise. Specific learning outcomes include: • Identify and discuss cutting-edge cancer research in different disciplines • Synthesize new research findings to a multidisciplinary audience. • Develop critical thinking skills: evaluate and analyze the results and methods used in inter-disciplinary cancer research   For Pillar 4 – Psychosocial Oncology, course content may include: • Research on the psychological, behavioral, emotional, and social issues affecting people with cancer and their caregivers throughout the disease trajectory i.e., from diagnosis to end of life. This includes distress screening, symptom management, and survivorship care planning. • Research addressing specific concerns such as fear of cancer recurrence, long-term adaptation after treatment, and the impact of health communication, patient–provider relationships, and shared decision-making that impacts psychological well-being. • Research on the effectiveness of psychosocial intervention to improve quality of life e.g., psychotherapy, support groups, meaning-centered therapy, cognitive-behavioral therapy, mindfulness, and clinical hypnosis, all in improving patient and caregiver outcomes. • Research on primary and secondary prevention from a psychosocial perspective, including promoting healthy behaviors, reducing risk factors, enhancing screening participation, and improving adherence to early detection and follow-up care.   Duties and Responsibilities The Course Lecturer will: • Serve as Course Coordinator for Pillar 4 - Psychosocial Oncology, contributing to course leadership alongside three co-coordinators (one per pillar). • Prepare and deliver Pillar - specific course content, or identify and invite guest lecturers, and select relevant research papers. • Attend sessions led by Pillar 4 guest lecturers. • Coordinate and communicate regularly with the other course coordinators to ensure alignment of learning objectives, course structure, and assessment strategies. • Contribute to the development and refinement of the course syllabus and learning activities, in collaboration with co-coordinators. • Evaluation of student work for Pillar 4, in accordance with McGill assessment policies. • Provide reasonable student consultation and hold office hours as required • Administer course-related responsibilities as required under the Course Lecturer Agreement (e.g., grading, review meetings, course feedback) • Attend assigned course sessions, including student presentations   Qualifications Required Qualifications • PhD or equivalent terminal degree in a field relevant to psychosocial oncology • Demonstrated expertise in Pillar 4–aligned research and/or professional practice • Evidence of teaching experience or training at the undergraduate or graduate level • Strong communication, organizational, and collaboration skills   Preferred Qualifications • Experience teaching or coordinating graduate-level courses • Familiarity with interdisciplinary or team-taught course models • Experience working with students from diverse academic and disciplinary backgrounds O t he r Fluency in English and French (written and verbal) preferred] McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Interdepartmental Studies Course Title: Seminars in Cancer Series Subject Code: CANC 621D2 D2 Location: Education Schedule: Thursday 9:05-10:55 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-29 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Birks-Building/Grader---RELG-358---Fall-2026_JR0000080699-1": {
      "title": "Grader - RELG 358 - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The School of Religious Studies is recruiting one Grader for RELG 358: Religion and Cinema in India, for the fall 2026 semester. Course Description: This class offers a survey of major 20th century films from India, ranging from early devotionals and Bollywood mythologicals to art house and parallel cinema masterpieces to explore key themes in the history and religion of India with a focus on 20th century developments. Thematic topics covered include identity, gender, visual culture, and representation in the study of modern South Asian religions through a focus on India in the twentieth century through the lens of film. Discussion focuses on a range of themes such as nationalism, devotion, and secularism. This class has no pre-requisites and assumes no prior knowledge. Job Description: Weekly grading of in-class writing assignments (53 submissions) and entering grades Weekly entry of student contribution to oral discussion in MyCourses register End of term grading of final projects and enter grades (submission on Dec 1, grades due on Dec 8) Assist with assigning and entering grades for Engagement 1 (mid-term time) and Engagement 2 (end of term) Assist with assigning and entering grades for In-Class Writing 1 (mid-term time) and In-class Writing 2 (end of term) Qualifications: MA or Ph.D. student, ideally has taken RELG 358 in the past or prior coursework or familiarity with South Asian Studies.  Hiring Unit: School of Religious Studies Course Title: RELG 358 - Religion and Cinema in India Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Estimated number of hours per term: 80 hours Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $25.00 Position Start Date: 2026-09-21 Position End Date: 2026-12-08 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-19 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/JGH---Jewish-General-Hosp/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Medicine--Division-of-Physical-Medicine-and-Rehabilitation--C3-260911-_JR0000080632": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical), Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (C3-260911)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description The McGill University Department of Medicine in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position in the Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical). The successful candidate will be based at Santé Québec Centre-Ouest-de-l'Île-de-Montréal – Universitaire, with primary clinical responsibilities at the Constance-Lethbridge site of the Lethbridge-Layton-Mackay (LLM) Rehabilitation Centre. The professorial rank will be determined according to the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience. This is a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. The Department of Medicine is a leader in academic medicine. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs across our teaching hospitals.  Constance Lethbridge is one of McGill’s major rehabilitation teaching sites affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec. The teams at LLM are dedicated to working with clients to fully maximize their autonomy and participation in the community through specialized and ultra-specialized rehabilitation services to adults living with physical disabilities across a broad range of neurological, musculoskeletal, and complex rehabilitation conditions while also supporting their families.   Job Duties Please see the Department of Medicine (DOM) website for McGill DOM Career path definitions. Our career path definitions specify typical ranges of time allotted to clinical work, teaching (clinical and didactic), research and administration. As a Clinician-Teacher, the successful candidate’s major non-clinical focus will be on teaching the next generation of physicians. Teaching activity will include both didactic and clinical teaching. As a Clinician-Educator, you will focus on education research of sufficient caliber to publish in peer-reviewed journals or education administration with a focus on leading/developing world-class training programs. The successful candidate will be assigned to the career path that best matches their qualifications and experience. Clinical duties will include consultation, comprehensive functional assessment, diagnosis, treatment planning, and longitudinal follow-up for individuals with complex rehabilitation needs. Working within an interdisciplinary rehabilitation environment, the successful candidate will provide medical expertise and collaborate with rehabilitation professionals to optimize function, mobility, participation, pain management, and community reintegration. Clinical activities will include consultation and follow-up for individuals with: •          Stroke and acquired brain injury; •          Traumatic brain injury; •          Chronic neurological conditions; •          Post-polio syndrome; •          Neuro-musculoskeletal conditions; •          Chronic pain, including complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS); •          Severe motor impairments and mobility limitations; •          Complex mobility, positioning, and specialized assistive technology needs. Clinical interventions may include: •          Spasticity assessment and management, including injection therapies; •          Musculoskeletal and neuromuscular rehabilitation management; •          Ultrasound-guided and other image-guided peripheral joint injections; •          Prescription of orthoses, assistive devices, and adaptive equipment; •          Return-to-work planning and vocational rehabilitation support; •          Integration of tele-rehabilitation and virtual care approaches, where appropriate. The successful candidate will contribute to quality improvement, program development, clinical pathway design, program evaluation, and initiatives aimed at improving access, continuity of care, patient outcomes, and interdisciplinary rehabilitation practice. The position will support the continued development and expansion of physiatry services within Santé Québec Centre-Ouest-de-l'Île-de-Montréal – Universitaire, including initiatives that promote value-based rehabilitation care and the measurement of outcomes that matter to patients. The incumbent will also be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents and involvement in academic and administrative committees).   McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates must: ​​​​Hold an MD degree and have completed specialty training in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation;  ​Be certified by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent) in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation;  ​Be eligible for licensure in Quebec and certification by the Collège des médecins du Québec; Have completed at least one additional year of specialty clinical training in neurological rehabilitation. Additional training or experience in clinical research is considered an asset;   ​Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families and health care professionals.​  Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant/Associate Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of date of posting The following supporting documents are required: A cover letter & curriculum vitae; A statement indicating your academic plans if you are successful in obtaining this position. Indicate your planned career path as a Clinician-Teacher or Clinician-Educator. Describe your clinical time allocation and detail your teaching plans and plans for service to the academic community; The names and contact information of three referees. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Invigilator---MATH-242--F26-_JR0000080652": {
      "title": "Invigilator – MATH 242 (F26)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Department of Mathematics and Statistics Exam Period : • MATH 242 - October 19th 2026 (7 :00pm-9:00pm plus setup and takedown) Position Summary : • Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. • Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. • Handles students' problems. • Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. • Alerts professor when students have a question. • At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). • Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensure delivery of the exams. Qualifications : • Must be client-focused and service oriented. • Punctual. • Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately. • Demonstrates ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems. • Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team. • Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams. • English, spoken and read. • French an asset. Experience and education : • High-School Diploma is the minimum requirement • Priority will be given to candidates who are already in McGill's priority pool Location: This position is located in Montreal, Quebec, and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec. Salary : • $18.00/hour 11.02 – “An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their Supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University.” Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Day-ChancellorHall/Student-Ambassadors-Ambassadeurs-tudiantes----AMM---EDI622_JR0000080690": {
      "title": "Student Ambassadors/Ambassadeurs étudiant.e.s  - AMM - EDI622",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Student Ambassadors – Admissions Office at the Faculty of Law at McGill 2026-2027 Ambassadeurs étudiant.e.s – Services des admissions à la Faculté de droit à McGill 2026-2027 The Faculty of Law’s Admissions Office is seeking to fill five part-time positions during the academic year for McGill law students. Candidates will be trained by and work with the Associate Dean (Admissions and Recruitment) and members of the Admissions Office team. Le Service des admissions de la Faculté de droit cherche à combler 5 postes d'emploi à temps partiel pendant l’année scolaire pour des étudiant(e)(s) en droit à McGill. Les candidat(e)(s) seront formé(e)s par et travailleront avec la Doyenne adjointe (admissions et recrutement) ainsi que les membres de l’équipe du Service des admissions. What is the Student Ambassador Program? This program connects admitted and prospective candidates with current law students. Ambassadors are uniquely placed to tell applicants about the advantages of studying law at McGill. In fact, many of our current ambassadors were themselves welcomed to the McGill Law community through this program. Student Ambassadors are also invited to attend alumni events and to participate in Homecoming or other semi-formal or formal functions. Qu'est-ce que le programme des étudiants ambassadeurs ? Ce programme met en relation les candidats admis et les futurs étudiants avec des étudiants en droit actuellement inscrits. Les ambassadeurs sont particulièrement bien placés pour présenter aux candidats les avantages d'étudier le droit à McGill. En effet, bon nombre de nos ambassadeurs actuels ont eux-mêmes été accueillis au sein de la communauté de la Faculté de droit de McGill grâce à ce programme. Les étudiants ambassadeurs sont également invités à assister à des événements organisés par les anciens élèves et à participer au Homecoming ou à d'autres réceptions semi-formelles ou formelles Position Summary Under the direct supervision of the Associate Dean (Admissions and Recruitment), the student ambassador will perform duties related to the recruitment of applicants and admitted students to the BCL/JD program. As representatives of the Faculty of Law, student ambassadors share their unique perspectives as current law students regarding the application and admissions process, the transition to life as a law student, and their knowledge of what McGill’s unique program has to offer. Résumé de la Position Sous la supervision directe de la Doyenne adjointe (admissions et recrutement), l'ambassadeur étudiant.e accomplira des tâches liées au recrutement de candidat.e.s et d'étudiant.e.s admis.es au programme BCL/JD. En tant que représentant.e.s de la Faculté de droit, les ambassadeurs étudiant.e.s partagent leurs perspectives uniques en tant qu'étudiant.e.s en droit actuel.le.s en ce qui concerne le processus de demande et d'admission, la transition vers le rôle d’etudiant.e en droit, ainsi que leurs connaissances de ce que le programme unique de McGill a à offrir. Description of Duties Greets students and answers general inquiries about the Faculty of Law (minors, majors, and joint degree programs, student life, living in Montreal, etc.) with a positive attitude. Hosts virtual and, when safety guidelines permit, in-person Faculty tours and class visits. In collaboration with the Faculty of Law’s Admissions Office, shares their student experience and answers questions from prospective and admitted students, their families, guidance counselors, and alumni during webinars, virtual events and, when safety guidelines permit, in-person events. In collaboration with the Faculty of Law’s Communications team, participates in social media campaigns and events (e.g. Instagram, Facebook and YouTube). In collaboration with the Faculty of Law’s Career Development Office, helps host on-campus events for prospective employers. Supports other recruitment and yield activities and events, as needed, such as answering the phone, responding to e-mails and assisting in various administrative tasks; helping with the setting up and taking down signs, equipment, etc. during on-campus events. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. Duration of Employment: Fall 2026 – Summer 2027 Weekly Hours: Weekly hours vary depending on event schedule and tasks assigned. When assigned, work will be 2 – 5 hours per week. Flexibility required. Qualifying Skills: Passive bilingualism General knowledge of the Faculty and student life Strong written and oral communication skills Strong interpersonal skills - must be willing to engage and initiate conversations with students and their families Involvement in the McGill community (clubs, services, athletics or other universityactivities) an asset Customer service and social media experience an asset All chosen Student Ambassadors will be required to complete a training given by the Admissions Office. Candidates should be bilingual, have facilitation and presentation experience and the ability to interact with various constituencies and groups. Prior experience with the Student Ambassador program or another program of its kind is a strong asset. Please upload your CV and short cover letter with the following information: Name and year of study (e.g., 1L, 2L, etc.) A brief bio/summary of your experiences prior to law Motivations for being a Student Ambassador Experience with work related to the duties described Plan for juggling other planned obligations/commitments for this next year alongside Student Ambassador work The 2nd document should not be more than 1 page and replaces the need for a cover letter. Description des tâches • Accueillir les étudiants et répondre aux demandes générales concernant la Faculté de droit (mineures, majeures et programmes de diplômes conjoints, vie étudiante, vie à Montréal, etc.) avec une attitude positive. • Animer des visites virtuelles de la Faculté et, lorsque les consignes de sécurité le permettent, des visites en personne ainsi que des visites de cours. • En collaboration avec le Bureau des admissions de la Faculté de droit, partager son expérience étudiante et répondre aux questions des étudiants potentiels et admis, de leurs familles, des conseillers en orientation et des diplômés lors de webinaires, d’événements virtuels et, lorsque les consignes de sécurité le permettent, d’événements en personne. • En collaboration avec l’équipe des communications de la Faculté de droit, participer à des campagnes et à des activités sur les médias sociaux (p. ex. Instagram, Facebook et YouTube). • En collaboration avec le Bureau du développement professionnel de la Faculté de droit, contribuer à l’organisation d’activités sur le campus destinées aux employeurs potentiels. • Soutenir, au besoin, d’autres activités et événements de recrutement et de conversion des candidats, notamment en répondant au téléphone, aux courriels et en effectuant diverses tâches administratives; contribuer à l’installation et au démontage des affiches, du matériel, etc. lors des événements sur le campus. • La liste des fonctions décrites ci-dessus est représentative et ne constitue pas une liste complète et détaillée des tâches qui peuvent être confiées à la personne titulaire du poste. Durée d’emploi Automne 2026 – été 2027 Heures hebdomadaires Les heures hebdomadaires varient en fonction de l’horaire des événements et des tâches assignées. Lorsqu’un travail est assigné, celui-ci représente de 2 à 5 heures par semaine. Flexibilité requise. Compétences recherchées • Bilinguisme passif • Connaissance générale de la Faculté et de la vie étudiante • Excellentes aptitudes en communication orale et écrite • Excellentes aptitudes interpersonnelles — la personne doit être à l’aise d’interagir avec les étudiants et leurs familles et de prendre l’initiative d’engager la conversation avec eux • Participation à la vie de la communauté mcgilloise (clubs, services, activités sportives ou autres activités universitaires), un atout • Expérience en service à la clientèle et dans les médias sociaux, un atout • Toutes les personnes retenues comme ambassadeurs étudiants devront suivre une formation offerte par le Bureau des admissions. Les candidats doivent être bilingues, posséder de l’expérience en animation et en présentation, ainsi que la capacité d’interagir avec différents publics et groupes. Une expérience antérieure dans le cadre du programme d’ambassadeurs étudiants ou d’un programme similaire constitue un atout important. Documents à soumettre Veuillez téléverser votre CV ainsi qu’une courte lettre de présentation contenant les renseignements suivants : • Nom et année d’études (p. ex. 1L, 2L, etc.) • Une courte biographie ou un résumé de vos expériences avant vos études en droit • Vos motivations à devenir ambassadeur étudiant • Votre expérience pertinente en lien avec les tâches décrites ci-dessus • Votre plan pour concilier vos autres obligations et engagements prévus au cours de la prochaine année avec votre travail d’ambassadeur étudiant Le deuxième document ne doit pas dépasser une page et remplace la nécessité de fournir une lettre de présentation distincte. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma Minimal or No Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE A) $17.17 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-08-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-22 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Tenure-Track-Position-in-Accounting--Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080360": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Accounting, Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Tenure-Track Position in Accounting Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Assistant Professor tenure-track position in the Accounting Area. The start date for the position is August 1, 2027 . The position is open to candidates with a Ph.D. in any area of specialization in Accounting , as well as to applicants demonstrating outstanding potential who are doctoral students and who plan to have completed the requirements for their Ph.D. by December 2027. Preference will be given to candidates whose research aligns with Faculty priority areas of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies; Innovation & Entrepreneurship; Sustainability; or Equity, Diversity & Inclusion. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses in financial accounting and management accounting and will demonstrate the ability to advance a cutting-edge, compelling and impactful research agenda. The ideal candidate will have evidence of effective teaching, an experience-appropriate record of research publications in high quality, peer-reviewed journals, and clear promise of conducting innovative and important research publishable in the top journals in accounting studies such as those listed on the Desautels 22 . Successful candidates are also expected to work actively with doctoral students, teach in our undergraduate and graduate programs, and contribute to the administration of the Area and Faculty. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. The Accounting Area at Desautels brings together scholars specializing in the creation and dissemination of high-quality accounting research. Professors have diverse disciplinary backgrounds and theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high level of independence in their research and teaching endeavors. McGill's emphasis is on research quality and academic and practitioner impact. The Faculty offers a Major and Concentration in Accounting at the undergraduate level, a joint honours program with Economics, and a Graduate Certificate in Professional Accounting (GCPA), an accredited CPA Quebec Professional Education Program preparing students to write the national Common Final Exam. The Area is also home to a PhD specialization in Accounting. Members of the Area are affiliated with the Business and Management Research Centre, a research incubator that helps develop and fund research initiatives through PhD seed grants, travel grants, and brownbag seminars. Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighbourhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for both individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world       Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels, teaching load, research funding and travel support are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday ( all in the same section as you submit your CV ): Cover letter Curriculum vitae Statement of current and prospective research When applicable, evidence of teaching effectiveness Sample research papers We are not requesting reference letters at this point; a reference check is a secondary step which may follow. All application materials should be submitted electronically by October 20, 2026 and only complete electronic submissions are accepted. The hiring process, including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the position is filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to wuyang.zhao@mcgill.ca The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Prof. Wuyang Zhao Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Redpath-Library/Communications-Assistant-B--Scribe-_JR0000074054-1": {
      "title": "Communications Assistant B (Scribe)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Student Accessibility and Achievement Position Summary: The role of a scribe is to support the exam process for a student who is taking their exams with accommodations at Student Accessibility and Achievement. The scribe is responsible for the physical writing/reading of exam responses on behalf of the student dictating their answers. Scribes are responsible for writing down verbatim what a student dictates during an examination, and reading aloud the response, if requested, for student verification of the scribed text. A scribe is required to have legible handwriting and be proficient in English in order to provide an accurate and legible record (handwritten or typed) from the personal dictation of a student in examination. A scribe does not provide clarification, guidance or opinion during the exam writing process. The scribe may ask the student to spell non-common and discipline specific terminology. Scribes may be asked to read back material to the student on a one-to-one basis. Scribes might also be asked to draw diagrams and use symbols. Therefore, depending on the subject area and if deemed appropriate by Student Accessibility and Achievement, a scribe may be required to have a background in the subject area they are scribing for. Primary Duties   To provide support to the examination process  To provide support to students with exam accommodations at Student Accessibility and Achievement   Escorting students from exam rooms during the examination as required (e.g: washrooms)  Ensuring the examination room is clear and tidied for the next session  To support student career readiness, we encourage supervisors to additionally describe learning outcomes/skills students can expect to develop in this role.  Produce an accurate and legible written (handwritten or computer) record of the student’s dictation. Proficient typing skills are required if typing a student’s dictated answers   Remain neutral throughout the exam – not alter the content produced by the student in any way  Produce maps, graphs or diagrams according to the student’s instructions (depending on academic field)   Read back the written answers to the student, as requested   Ensure exam integrity and security and adhere to exam regulations   Report incidents such as disturbances or cheating   Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Ability to exercise discretion and confidentiality at all times Superior and clear communication and listening skills Have a positive attitude towards equality and access. Be sensitive and respectful to the needs of students with various learning styles and from diverse educational, social and cultural backgrounds Be reliable, punctual and respect professional boundaries at all times Accurate and proficient typing and/or legible handwriting skills (sample may be required) Ability to write verbatim only what the test-taker says Responsive to email communication Ability to read aloud clearly, at a normal pace, with good pronunciation, verbatim only the words in the test Adhering to McGill University policies and procedures Ability to work independently Accuracy and attention to detail Ability to keep calm under pressure Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (written, fluent) and at a level 4 (oral, fluent) on a scale of 0-4. Education/Experience Required   Current McGill student (senior undergraduate and graduate students preferred)  High English proficiency required  Expected Start Date: August 31, 2026 Hourly Rate:   $18/hr  Hours of work:    Hours of work cannot be guaranteed. This is an “on-call” position, work hours vary and depend upon need.    Partake in a 30-minute mandatory (paid) training session by Student Accessibility and Achievement pertaining to the role.    Any questions about the roles and responsibilities of a scribe during an examination setting can be directed to the Casual Staffing and Operations Manager, Bhoomasri Gururajan, at: bhoomasri.gururajan@mcgill.ca or  Udit Tyagi at: udit.tyagi@mcgill.ca Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $18.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 3 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-07-23 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Arts-Building/Assistant-Professor_JR0000080515": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Faculty of Arts at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position to advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the following strategic priority area: Democratic and community resilience Emerging Leaders must have an independent program of research that demonstrates strategic alignment with this priority area. The award provides $100,000 per year for six years to McGill University to help support the salary costs associated with the successful Emerging Leader appointment as well as direct research expenses, with the possibility of extension for a further six years. Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment in the Department of Art History and Communication Studies at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 9, 2027 . Job Duties The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader will develop and pursue a research program aligned with the democratic and community resilience priority area. The successful candidate will also teach undergraduate and graduate courses, develop courses in their areas of specialization, supervise and mentor graduate students in the Department of Art History and Communication Studies, and contribute to the intellectual life of the University. Democratic and community resilience refers to the capacity of political institutions and communities to withstand and respond to pressures including rising authoritarianism and militarism, increasing economic precarity, declining trust in news media, environmental injustice, gendered, ableist, racist and other forms of prejudice and violence, and deteriorating conditions for diversity, equality, and freedom. Emerging communication and media technologies, including social media platforms, proliferating surveillance systems, cryptocurrencies, prediction markets, algorithmic decision-making and accelerating Artificial Intelligence are implicated in these pressures. A Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader will develop critical research and teaching programs that address the social, cultural, political, economic, infrastructural and environmental dimensions of emerging media, across regional, national and global contexts. Successful candidates will demonstrate their potential to contribute to intentional media policy, regulation and governance in the public interest, robust artistic and creative cultures, and/or vibrant community and political engagement with emerging media technologies. We seek candidates whose research centers the priorities, experiences and knowledges of historically and structurally disadvantaged and marginalized people, communities and regions, and who are committed to serving a diverse student body and an inclusive campus climate. Qualifications and Education Requirements Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 9, 2026, are eligible. Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible. Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently. Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. The category and period of eligible leaves should be clearly indicated in the required cover letter. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited. A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself. Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying .   Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Application Process Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system, using the link below. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday . Refer to the How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) job aid for instructions on how to apply. Required Documents The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB. Cover letter Curriculum vitae (including links to representative publications) Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (max. 1 page) Teaching statement and dossier (max. 1 page) Contact information for three references  Job Details Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience Posting Period Posting date: Wednesday September 16, 2026 Closing date: 30 days from posting date Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to Darin Barney, Professor and Chair, Department of Art History and Communication Studies ( darin.barney@mcgill.ca ). Commitment to Equity and Diversity Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . _____________________________________________________________ La Faculté des arts de l’Université McGill invite les candidatures pour un poste à temps plein de professeur adjoint menant à la permanence, destiné à soutenir le développement d’un programme de recherche transformationnel contribuant au rayonnement et au leadership international du Canada dans le domaine prioritaire stratégique suivant : Résilience démocratique et résilience des communautés La personne candidate doit disposer d’un programme de recherche indépendant démontrant un lien clair et stratégique avec ce domaine prioritaire. Le programme prévoit une contribution de 100 000 $ par année pendant six ans à l’Université McGill afin de soutenir les coûts salariaux associés à la nomination de la personne retenue ainsi que les dépenses directes de recherche. Une prolongation pouvant aller jusqu’à six années supplémentaires pourrait être accordée. La personne retenue devra entrer en fonction dans un poste à temps plein de professeur adjoint menant à la permanence au Département d’histoire de l’art et d’études en communication , au plus tard le 9 juin 2027 . Responsabilités La personne recrutée dans le cadre des chaires de recherche Impact+ Canada devra élaborer et mener un programme de recherche en lien avec le domaine prioritaire de la r ésilience démocratique et communautaire. Elle devra également enseigner aux niveaux du premier cycle et des cycles supérieurs, concevoir et développer des cours correspondant à ses domaines de spécialisation, superviser, encadrer et accompagner des étudiants et étudiantes aux cycles supérieurs au sein du Département d’histoire de l’art et d’études en communication, et contribuer activement à la vie intellectuelle et universitaire de McGill. La résilience démocratique et communautaire renvoie à la capacité des institutions politiques et des communautés à résister à différentes formes de pression et à y répondre, notamment la montée de l’autoritarisme et du militarisme, l’accroissement de la précarité économique, l’érosion de la confiance envers les médias d’information, les injustices environnementales, les préjugés et les violences fondés notamment sur le genre, le capacitisme et le racisme, ainsi que la dégradation des conditions favorisant la diversité, l’égalité et la liberté. Les technologies émergentes de communication et les nouveaux médias — notamment les plateformes de médias sociaux, la multiplication des systèmes de surveillance, les cryptomonnaies, les marchés prédictifs, la prise de décision algorithmique et le développement rapide de l’intelligence artificielle — jouent un rôle dans ces transformations et ces tensions. La personne retenue dans le cadre de Canada Impact+ devra développer des programmes de recherche et d’enseignement critiques portant sur les dimensions sociales, culturelles, politiques, économiques, infrastructurelles et environnementales des médias émergents, dans des contextes régionaux, nationaux et internationaux. Les candidatures seront particulièrement considérées en fonction du potentiel de la personne à contribuer à l’élaboration de politiques publiques, à la réglementation et à la gouvernance des médias dans l’intérêt public, au développement de cultures artistiques et créatives dynamiques, et/ou à la participation communautaire et politique autour des technologies médiatiques émergentes. Nous recherchons des personnes dont les recherches accordent une place centrale aux priorités, aux expériences et aux savoirs de personnes, de communautés et de régions historiquement et structurellement défavorisées ou marginalisées. Les candidates et candidats doivent également être engagés envers un milieu universitaire inclusif et envers l’accompagnement d’un corps étudiant diversifié. Qualifications et exigences Pour être admissibles, les candidates et candidats doivent avoir été établis à l’étranger, y travailler et y résider le 9 juin 2026. Les candidates et candidats doivent se situer dans les cinq années suivant le début de leur première nomination liée à la recherche, après déduction de la durée de toute interruption admissible de carrière en recherche. Les personnes récemment diplômées ainsi que les stagiaires postdoctoraux peuvent également être admissibles. Une nomination liée à la recherche est une nomination dans le cadre de laquelle la personne dispose de l’autonomie nécessaire pour mener des recherches de façon indépendante. Les congés admissibles — notamment les congés de maternité, parentaux, médicaux et de deuil — sont comptabilisés à deux fois leur durée réelle. La nature et la durée de tout congé admissible doivent être clairement indiquées dans la lettre de présentation. Les congés professionnels, notamment les congés de formation, sabbatiques ou administratifs, ne sont pas pris en compte. L’Université McGill exige un doctorat (Ph. D.) pour une nomination à un poste menant à la permanence. Toutefois, le doctorat ne constitue pas une exigence du programme des chaires de recherche Impact+ Canada Canada lui-même. Les candidates et candidats sont invités à consulter les critères d’ admissibilité et de sélection du programme Impact+ avant de présenter leur candidature. Les Canadiennes et Canadiens vivant à l’étranger qui souhaitent s’établir au Canada sont encouragés à présenter leur candidature. Processus de candidature Les candidatures doivent être soumises par l’intermédiaire de Workday, le système de gestion des ressources humaines de l’Université McGill, en utilisant le lien fourni ci-dessous. Lors de la création d’un compte Workday, veuillez utiliser une adresse courriel personnelle . Pour connaître les étapes à suivre, veuillez consulter le guide How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) . Documents exigés Le dossier de candidature doit inclure les documents suivants, regroupés en un seul fichier PDF d’une taille maximale de 5 Mo : une lettre de présentation; un curriculum vitæ, comprenant des liens vers des publications représentatives; un énoncé de recherche présentant les travaux actuels et futurs, leur adéquation avec le domaine prioritaire stratégique ainsi que leur potentiel en matière de traduction, de mobilisation et d’application des résultats de recherche ( 2 pages maximum ); une description des besoins en infrastructures de recherche , notamment en matière d’espace de laboratoire et d’équipement ( 1 page maximum ); un énoncé et dossier d’enseignement ( 1 page maximum ); les coordonnées de trois personnes pouvant fournir des références . Conditions du poste Type de poste : Poste menant à la permanence Rang : Professeur adjoint Salaire : Selon les qualifications et l’expérience. Période d’affichage Date d’affichage : Mercredi 16 septembre 2026 Date limite de candidature : 30 jours après la date d’affichage Les demandes d’information de la part de candidates et candidats potentiels sont les bienvenues et seront traitées en toute confidentialité. Les personnes intéressées peuvent communiquer avec Darin Barney, Professeur et directeur, Département d’histoire de l’art et d’études en communication, Université McGill ( darin.barney@mcgill.ca ). Engagement envers l’équité, la diversité et l’inclusion Pour travailler à l’Université McGill, vous devez être autorisé à travailler au Canada et être disposé à travailler au Québec, sur le campus où le poste est situé. L’Université McGill offre un soutien en matière d’immigration et de relocalisation aux personnes retenues. L’Université McGill est une université de langue anglaise. La majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’y déroulent en anglais; les personnes candidates doivent donc être en mesure de communiquer efficacement en anglais, tant à l’oral qu’à l’écrit. L’Université McGill est résolument engagée en faveur de l’équité et de la diversité au sein de sa communauté et accorde une grande importance à la rigueur et à l’excellence universitaires. Nous encourageons notamment les candidatures de personnes racisées et de minorités visibles, de femmes, de personnes autochtones, de personnes en situation de handicap, de membres de minorités ethniques et de personnes appartenant à des minorités en matière d’orientation sexuelle ou d’identité de genre. Toutes les personnes qualifiées possédant les compétences et les connaissances nécessaires pour contribuer de manière constructive à des communautés diversifiées sont également invitées à postuler. McGill valorise et encourage les recherches qui s’appuient sur une diversité de traditions intellectuelles, de méthodologies et de modes de diffusion et de mobilisation des connaissances. Les candidates et candidats sont invités à mettre en évidence les retombées de leurs travaux, tant à l’intérieur de leur discipline et entre différentes disciplines qu’auprès d’autres secteurs, notamment les gouvernements, les communautés et l’industrie. McGill reconnaît également l’incidence que peuvent avoir certains congés — notamment pour des raisons familiales ou de santé — sur la progression d’une carrière universitaire. Les candidates et candidats sont encouragés à signaler tout congé ayant pu entraîner une diminution de leur productivité ou avoir une incidence sur leur parcours professionnel. Ces renseignements seront pris en compte afin de favoriser une évaluation équitable du dossier. L’Université McGill applique un programme d’équité en matière d’emploi et encourage les membres des groupes désignés à s’auto-identifier. Elle s’efforce également de garantir le traitement équitable et la pleine inclusion des personnes en situation de handicap, notamment par l’application transversale des principes de conception universelle à l’ensemble de la vie universitaire ainsi que par ses politiques et procédures d’accommodement. Les personnes en situation de handicap qui prévoient avoir besoin de mesures d’adaptation à quelque étape que ce soit du processus de candidature peuvent communiquer en toute confidentialité avec Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
