    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Buildings-and-Research-Infrastructure-Manager_JR0000080459": {
      "title": "Buildings and Research Infrastructure Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). This permanent position is based in the Dean's Office, Faculty of Engineering. Position Summary: The Buildings & Research Infrastructure Manager provides professional expertise , planning, and oversight for research infrastructure, physical space development, and building operations in the Faculty of Engineering. The incumbent ensures that Faculty buildings, laboratories, and research environments meet University standards for safety, compliance, functionality, and service quality.   Working closely with Facilities Management and Ancillary Services (FMAS), Campus Planning & Development Office (CPDO), Environmental Health & Safety (EHS), Design Services, Project Management, the Office of the McGill Sustainability Park , and the Office of Sponsored Research (OSR), the incumbent manages infrastructure projects, supports researchers undertaking laboratory renovations, and ensures alignment with Faculty priorities, regulatory requirements, and budget constraints.   The role oversees space allocation, building operations, and maintains comprehensive data on Faculty infrastructure needs and priorities. Primary Responsibilities: Oversee Faculty-wide planning and management of research infrastructure, laboratory development, and physical space, including space allocation, utilization analysis, renovation planning, and optimization initiatives, ensuring alignment with Faculty priorities and institutional standards. Identify infrastructure and space needs, risks, and opportunities, and provide recommendations to support effective resource allocation, capital planning, and long-term infrastructure development. Oversee Faculty-managed building operations, ensuring effective maintenance, service delivery, operational efficiency, and compliance with University standards. Oversee research infrastructure and renovation projects in collaboration with Principal Investigators, the Project Management Office, and other stakeholders by defining project scope, objectives, timelines, budgets, and deliverables. Oversee progress, manage risks, resolve issues; and ensure timely, cost-effective project completion. Provide expert advice and technical guidance to researchers and Faculty leadership on laboratory design, space planning, infrastructure requirements, project feasibility, and applicable safety, regulatory, and funding agency requirements. Develop, oversee, and analyze project budgets, cost estimates, financial forecasts, and funding requirements, identifying risks and providing recommendations to support informed decision-making. Serve as the primary liaison among researchers, service units, regulatory bodies, funding agencies, and project stakeholders, fostering collaboration and supporting successful infrastructure planning, implementation, reporting, and compliance. Ensure compliance with applicable safety, environmental, regulatory, and funding agency requirements, including University policies and external regulations, in collaboration with EHS, FMAS, the Fire Prevention Office, the Office of Sponsored Research, and other relevant stakeholders. Represent the Faculty on committees and working groups related to laboratory safety, building operations, research infrastructure, and institutional planning priorities. Oversee logistics related to the relocation of Faculty of Engineering researchers to the McGill Sustainability Park (MSP), in collaboration with the MSP team and Director of Administration Supervise and provide leadership to the Building Administrator positions. Minimum Education & Experience Professional designation or undergraduate degree in engineering, architecture, or a related field; Master’s degree preferred. Several years of relevant experience in a public‑sector or institutional environment. Experience in facilities‑related project management, laboratory infrastructure, and construction practices in Québec. Other Qualifying Skills and Abilities Strong problem‑solving, negotiation, and diplomatic skills. Excellent organizational skills and ability to manage multiple projects under pressure. Client‑focused approach with strong interpersonal and communication skills. Extensive knowledge of laboratory infrastructure, safety requirements, and research environments. Familiarity with Québec construction industry practices and regulatory frameworks. Experience with project management software. Demonstrated ability to prepare budgets, forecasts, and financial analyses. Ability to orchestrate, document, and report on multiple concurrent projects. Fluency in English and French, spoken and written. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-LOG2A - Facilities Operations & Maintenance - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Administration Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Adams-Building/Student-Advising-Administrator_JR0000080455": {
      "title": "Student Advising Administrator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Faculty of Science Science Office of Undergraduate Student Advising (SOUSA) Position Summary: The Student Advising Administrator will join a team of administrative and advising professionals working in the Science Office of Undergraduate Student Advising (SOUSA).  Together, we provide guidance and support to over 6000 undergraduate students; assisting them in making informed academic choices in light of their learning, career and life goals. Reporting to the Associate Director, Student Affairs, the Student Advising Administrator provides administrative support around academic advising and student affairs services to undergraduate students in the Faculty of Science and the Bachelor of Arts and Science.  The incumbent evaluates and makes decisions regarding readmission, transfers, exchange and study away.  This role also supports the smooth functioning of the unit’s operations and acts as a resource person for policies and procedures. Primary Responsibilities: Work collaboratively as a member of the Student Affairs team to provide administrative support to undergraduate students across a range of academic and student affairs matters. Responds to general and complex inquiries, assists in resolving issues, and shares responsibility with other team members for Student Affairs activities and portfolios. Provide front-line support to students and responds to inquiries from students, parents, faculty, staff and other members of the University community. Assess the nature of inquiries, provide information and assistance, and refer individuals to the appropriate resource or office as required. Act as resource person regarding academic and administrative policies and procedures for undergraduate students in the Faculty of Science and the Bachelor of Arts & Science. Process requests related to the assessment of course equivalencies, recognition of courses from other institutions and transfer of credits. Advise outgoing and incoming exchange students and processes applications (in collaboration with Study Away Office) Evaluate requests for readmission and transfer, make decisions on routine cases.  Assess files, contact students and inform them of results and options.  Prepares files in more complex cases for senior advising consideration. Help produce, review and maintain unit websites, reports and documents (including publications, orientation materials, presentations and reference guides). Compile and produce reports for statistical and planning purposes and make recommendations on process improvements. Maintain and update student information systems and computerized applications and participate in their development to improve efficiency. This includes a range of computer programs and applications, including but not limited to: Microsoft Office (Outlook, OneDrive, Teams, Bookings, Forms, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Power Automate), Workday, Banner, Minerva, Adobe, Webex and Zoom. Participate in University orientation, McGill Open House and other recruitment and advising events. Undertaking special projects. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Bilingual; excellent verbal and written communication skills, in both English and French. Demonstrated experience in student affairs, student services, in support of academic advising, or a related role within a higher education environment. Ability to interpret and explain complex policies clearly and professionally. Strong ability to build rapport with students, faculty, and staff; tact and discretion when handling sensitive situations such as appeals or readmission refusals. Proven ability to prioritize tasks, coordinate meetings and events, perform under pressure and manage multiple responsibilities in a fast-paced and high-volume environment. Ability to assess student files, interpret academic policies, and make sound decisions on routine cases. Resourceful and proactive in resolving issues, proposing solutions, and supporting students through complex processes. Accuracy in maintaining records, preparing documentation, and updating advising resources. Capacity to work independently and collaboratively, handle confidential information, and adapt to changing priorities with tight deadlines. High level proficiency with MS Office Microsoft Office (Outlook, OneDrive, Teams, Bookings, Forms, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Power Automate), Workday, Banner, Minerva, Adobe, Webex and Zoom. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience with DEC III / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level H) $33.05 - $40.97 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director Student Affairs Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Accounting-Clerk-Secretary_JR0000080461": {
      "title": "Accounting Clerk/Secretary",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, this position performs a combination of accounting and administrative duties. Responsibilities include authorizing financial commitments according to established procedures, verifying, analyzing, and recording accounting transactions using automated financial systems, and investigating discrepancies as needed. The role also involves general office tasks such as typing documents, answering telephone calls, receiving visitors, updating information systems, and maintaining organized files. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Update and maintain files, schedule and book patient appointments Greet patients and process payments Answer phone calls and emails; handle routine correspondence process accounts receivable Process invoices and deposits Liaise with external vendors Prepare reports and other documents Perform various administrative and office support tasks Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Business Administration 2 Years Related Experience / DEP - Secretarial Studies As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Secretarial Studies 2 Years Related Experience / DEP - Business Administration Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level D) $26.18 - $32.46 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, Administration Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-09-15 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-29 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Program-Administrator_JR0000080557": {
      "title": "Program Administrator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Associate Director – Admissions & Recruitment, the Program Administrator provides administrative support to the MBA program with the primary focus on student orientation, scheduling, course related activities, and communications.    Primary Responsibilities: Provides support to new students by preparing orientation materials and guides, as well as scheduling and planning “Base Camp”  Coordinate MBA course scheduling by liaising with the infrastructure team to provide MBA course constraints and review draft schedules with the Academic director  Ensure the timetable and important dates documents are updated, as well as liaising with the E-calendar for MBA pages  Coordinate MBA core course administration, including instructor contracts, course evaluations, textbook/coursepack set-up, and submission and tracking of requests to Desautels HR for casual teaching support positions  Provide support to the Academic Director by monitoring teaching costs as well as any student requests for funding in comparison to the provided annual budget  Support administration of the Desautels Case Competition, including finding judges (with support from University Advancement), booking spaces, creating the myCourses page, ordering the case, creating the schedule with the academic in charge, and monitoring student registration  Support the admissions team as needed, attend the Masters Recruitment Committee to take minutes, and attend virtual recruitment events as required  Conduct one-on-one sessions to provide information on program pathways, switching programs, choice of electives, internships/practicums, etc.). and answer student questions via email  Receive Independent Study applications and register students. Using existing templates, create and submit Independent Study supervisor contracts  Produce the MBA Newsletter by collecting items from advisors and career coaches, formatting the information, selecting appropriate photos, and ensuring that Envoke licenses are renewed/paid annually.  Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities A self-starter with student affairs and admissions experience. Team player with a strong sense of commitment and dedication to team members and other stakeholders. Excellent organizational and communication skills with strong attention to detail. Ability to prioritize and manage several priorities at once. Knowledge of Slate and myCourses is a definite asset. May be required to shift their workday schedule occasionally to accommodate the off-campus schedule. Demonstrated ability to use the Microsoft Office suite (i.e. word, excel, teams) as well as demonstrated comfort with Banner and Minerva. English (spoken and written) French (spoken and written). As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience with DEC III / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level H) $33.05 - $40.97 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director - Admissions & Recruitment Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-11 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Cattle-Complex/Chief-Animal-Science-Technician_JR0000080526": {
      "title": "Chief Animal Science Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the   General-Manager , oversees the day-to-day running of the feed-room area in the Dairy complex .    Oversees the operation and maintenance of all equipment including motorized carts, silos and conveyors, manure systems (liquid and solid), and ventilation and milking systems .  Collaborates with the Animal Science Coordinator and the nutritionist to   determine   various rations for the dairy herd.   Major Duties and Responsibilities: - Remains available to be on a regular on-call rotation to address feed and feed room operations with staff after hours and on week-ends.  - Assigns tasks, trains staff in the operation of the feed room (e.g. preparation and feeding of rations, operation of equipment, routine maintenance), monitors progress and verifies work of several casual employees working on different schedules over 7 days per week.  Provides ongoing technical guidance and instruction on work methods, diet formulation, and feeding procedures.  Monitors feed inventory; prepares feed requirement forecasts; orders bagged & bulk feeds, & bedding.  Participates in certain physically demanding tasks such as filling and sealing silos, loading/unloading hay, straw, wood and paper bedding.  - Prepares feed rations for herd, or monitors rations preparation, feed quality, distribution to animals in accordance with schedule and prescribed quantities.   Provides support with research projects, at times with requirements to feed special diets, collect samples, examine and/or move animals, modify installations and accurately record data according to protocol.  - Monitors body condition of dairy herd, including lactating cows, dry cows and heifers.  Oversees management of pastures, including timely rotation of animals, fence maintenance, water and irrigation system and weed control.  Trains, assigns tasks, verifies the work of Animal Care Assistants carrying out rotational grazing and organizing pastures and irrigation system over 7 days per week from May to November.  - Conducts teaching labs for degree and diploma classes on topics such as ration formulation, feed storage, body condition scoring and monitoring, dehorning, and manure, ventilation and feeding systems.  Participates in instruction and training of student interns, and verifies their work with a written evaluation.  - Performs technical duties related to animal husbandry such as administration of medications, monitoring animal health, assisting with calving's, moving and restraining cows, routine animal care, various daily chores (sweeping, cleaning pens, replacing bedding, and milking when needed).  Assists with field work when needed.   - Oversees general maintenance of equipment in the Dairy unit.   Implements a regular maintenance schedule for equipment and maintains records of maintenance/repairs done.  Inspects and ensures functioning of equipment in milk house (e.g. pipeline, milking machines, bulk tank, compressors, containers of cleaning solutions and sanitizers), pumps, ventilation fans, silos, conveyors, feed carts, skid steer etc.   Operates equipment in the unit (feed carts, silo unloaders, skid steer, loader tractor, pressure washing equipment, etc.).  - Remains familiar with Dairy SOPs and ensures adherence to safety and waste management procedures.  Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Driver’s license   is mandatory.  Bilingual French and English (Level 3).    As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III - Animal Health Technology 3 Years Related Experience / DEC III - Farm Management Technology Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level G) $31.19 - $38.65 Hours per Week: 40 (Full time) Supervisor: General-Manager Campus Farm Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-04 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Senior-Career-Coach_JR0000080594": {
      "title": "Senior Career Coach",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Director, Career Services, the Senior Career Coach provides senior professional expertise and specialized-finance-career coaching for career development services supporting finance programs (MMF, MMFL, MBA – finance, and HIM). The incumbent drives the development of finance career education and employer engagement initiatives, ensuring alignment with Faculty priorities, labour market trends, employer expectations, and graduate employment outcomes. Acting as a senior subject matter and industry expert and advisor, works in close partnership with the MMF Career Coach, and supports Faculty leadership, academic program teams, employers, alumni, and career services staff through advanced finance coaching expertise, and the development of high-impact programming and partnerships. Primary Responsibilities: 1. Career Services Planning & Development Shape the approach for finance-career programming for MMF, MMFL, MBA (finance) and HIM (BCom), in close collaboration with the MMF Career Coach and aligned with Faculty and University priorities. Develop and implement short- and long-term plans to strengthen employment outcomes, employer engagement, student readiness, and overall service excellence. Conduct labor market and industry analysis to inform programming, employer outreach, and strategic priorities. Advise Academic Program Directors and Faculty Leadership regarding finance-related student employability, labor market conditions, curriculum alignment, and career-related initiatives, contributing to the Faculty’s reputation, rankings, and market positioning through graduate employability efforts. 2. Advanced Coaching, Industry Expertise & Program Leadership Provide specialized, high-level guidance to students pursuing careers in finance-related sectors, including advanced preparation for competitive recruitment processes, technical interviews, networking, and market-specific career pathways. Provide coaching to students and recent alumni on selecting career directions, complex career planning and employment matters. Lead the development and continuous enhancement of finance-specific career education programming aligned with employer expectations, technical interview demands, and student employment outcomes. Contribute finance-specific best practices, tools, and quality standards to enhance the Career Coach’s practice and strengthen consistency in student messaging across programs. 3. Employer Relations & Operational Oversight Represent the Faculty and Career Services team at senior-level meetings, employer engagements, and industry events. Collaborate with the Business Development team to cultivate strategic employer, alumni, and industry partnerships that expand recruitment opportunities and strengthen employer engagement. Provide oversight of employment analytics, reporting processes, and career services systems supporting rankings, accreditation, and continuous improvement initiatives. Recommend and implement improvements to systems, processes, and service delivery models. 4. Leadership & Collaboration Act as a senior resource to the Career Services team on finance-related career practices, market intelligence, and advanced technical interview preparation. Lead or participate in cross-functional initiatives related to student success, graduate employability, and employer engagement. Provide senior professional expertise and guidance to stakeholders across the Faculty including on finance labor markets trends, student employability, and curriculum alignment. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Minimum of ten (10) years of professional experience in the Finance sector, ideally in a variety of roles (corporate finance, banking, investment banking, etc.);  Demonstrated experience in senior-level career coaching, employer engagement, and stakeholder management. Experience working with graduate and undergraduate business students and employer recruitment environments. Extensive knowledge of labour market trends, recruitment practices, and career development strategies. Strong strategic thinking, leadership, communication, and relationship management skills. High degree of independent judgment and strategic decision-making in a complex and fast-paced environment. Proven ability to exercise sound judgment, manage confidential information with discretion, and operate effectively in a dynamic environment. High level of professionalism, initiative, and client-service orientation. Bilingual (French/English) required. The position averages approximately two (2) days per week over the course of the year, with workload fluctuations requiring increased availability during peak preparation and recruitment periods and reduced time commitments during quieter periods. Availability for occasional travel to support recruitment and career services activities and events. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience with Bachelor’s Degree / Master's Degree 2 Years Related Experience with Master’s Degree Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF3B - Student Services - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Supervisor: Associate Professor Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-09-27 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-29 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/School-of-Computer-Science-Teaching-Assistant-COMP-310-Fall-2026_JR0000077155": {
      "title": "School of Computer Science Teaching Assistant COMP 310 Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: School of Computer Science Course Title: Operating Systems Course Code: COMP 310 Estimated Number of Positions: 5 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Hiring Unit: School of Computer Science Course Number: COMP 310 - Course Title: Operating Systems Hours of work (per term): 90     Required duties: •    effectively and timely communicate with the instructor and the students; •    maintain and observe office and/or lab hours; •    develop grading scripts to be used to grade assignments and/or exams; •    grade assignments and exams under the supervision of the instructor; •    monitor the discussion board/emails; •    prepare solution sets for assignments, etc.; •    supervise students during midterm evaluations and/or quizzes; •    use software for plagiarism detection and verify similarities in the students' work; •    be available throughout the examination period; •    photo copy course notes, hand outs, etc.; •    ensure confidentiality regarding course-related matters; •    respect and treat all students equally. Qualifications Required: Must have command of the course material and must be able to demonstrate this command to the satisfaction of the course instructor. Must be willing to interact with students in the lab.  Must also have a good command of the English language and must possess good communication skills. Name of Immediate Supervisor: Max Kopinsky NOTES:  These positions are open to McGill Registered Graduate students only. •   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. •    Announcements are considered tentative, pending final determination of course offerings and enrolments.  Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-05-27 Position End Date: 2026-12-04 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-17 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Invigilator---MATH-133--F26-_JR0000080650": {
      "title": "Invigilator – MATH 133 (F26)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Department of Mathematics and Statistics Exam Period : • MATH 133 - October 19th 2026 (6 :15pm-8:15pm plus setup and takedown) Position Summary : • Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. • Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. • Handles students' problems. • Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. • Alerts professor when students have a question. • At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). • Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensure delivery of the exams. Qualifications : • Must be client-focused and service oriented. • Punctual. • Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately. • Demonstrates ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems. • Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team. • Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams. • English, spoken and read. • French an asset. Experience and education : • High-School Diploma is the minimum requirement • Priority will be given to candidates who are already in McGill's priority pool Location: This position is located in Montreal, Quebec, and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec. Salary : • $18.00/hour 11.02 – “An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their Supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University.” Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-26 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Outaouais-Campus-McGill/Invigilator---Campus-Outaouais_JR0000080756": {
      "title": "Invigilator - Campus Outaouais",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). *Exams will be held on Campus Outaouais in Gatineau, building location(s) to be confirmed* Hiring Unit: Campus Outaouais Number of Invigilators: 2 (estimated) Exam Period: Fall Semester (September 1, 2026 – December 31, 2026) Position Summary: Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. Administers the delivery of the electronic and paper exams and documentation accordingly. Handles students’ problems and directs them to IT support or Chief Exam Invigilator as needed. Responsible to report any behavior by students or others that does not comply with University regulations. Ensures that all mobile and web‐accessible electronic devices are collected from the students prior to the start of the examination and returned upon their departure. Ensures that students are accompanied to the washroom. Alerts professor or Chief Exam Administrator when students have a question. At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor or Chief Exam Administrator and ensuring delivery of the exams. *An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University, article 11.02.01. * If you are selected to be an Invigilator, please be advised that you will not yet be confirmed a shift. The allocation of a shift will be in accordance to article 13.01 in the Invigilation Collective Agreement whereby selected Invigilators will be confirmed a shift based on priority pool and availability. Other Qualifying Skills and Abilities: Must be client‐focused and service oriented. Punctual. Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately. Demonstrated ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems. Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team. Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams. English, spoken and read. French an asset. Qualifications: High School Leaving Certificate/Diploma (minimum requirement). Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-20 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Cattle-Complex/Animal-Care-Assistant-B-3_JR0000080698": {
      "title": "Animal Care Assistant B-3",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Macdonald Campus Farm/Dairy Unit Position Summary: Feeding /milking /cleaning pens / bedding stalls /sweeping /cleaning-brushing cows/  educational tours and all other duties assigned by the senior technician.  This is a high paced working environment, be prepared for physical work! You may also be required to work at other areas of the Farm as determined by the Farm Manager, doing a variety of tasks not necessarily listed in this contract. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Previous work on a dairy farm an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #2 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $18.63 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-09-21 Position End Date: 2027-01-09 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-22 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
