    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Brown-Student-Serv/Wellness-Access-Advisor_JR0000080611": {
      "title": "Wellness Access Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Student Wellness Hub provides a range of health services to McGill students with a focus on  awareness, prevention, and early intervention . The Student Wellness Hub has a variety of clinicians that provide one-on-one appointments, therapy groups and other services. Within the Student Wellness Hub, the Healthy Living Annex focuses on  health promotion  and offers  peer support  programs for both the Macdonald and downtown campuses.  Position Summary: Reporting directly to the Associate Director Wellness (Communities), the Wellness Access Advisor with mental health expertise serves a central role in facilitating access for students with mental health needs to appropriate levels of care at the appropriate time within an interdisciplinary and multidisciplinary setting. Primary Responsibilities: Conducts initial screening and assessment for students presenting with mental health and wellbeing concerns, during which they provide a rapid assessment, brief intervention, referral, education, and development of a wellness plan. Collects and reviews pertinent information/history, drawing on knowledge of mental health and counselling knowledge and skills when making an initial assessment through a structured judgment process.  Determines the need for emergent crisis intervention and facilitates urgent and non-urgent referrals. Initiates the Crisis Management Protocol, if needed. Makes appropriate referrals to a variety of student support services across campus. Maintains up-to-date and comprehensive information on student services and community resources across campus, including faculty-based and central student services (e.g. referral, best practices, policies and processes, etc.) as well as community based mental health services. Collaborates with referral sources as needed to streamline students’ access to support, including other mental health care providers, academic advisors, Enrolment Services Professionals, Residence Life Managers, Student Support Services Managers and the Dean of Students. Ensures the support & follow up of referrals. Creates and maintains student records including assessments and referrals as well as appointment information in accordance with established standards, policies and procedures and the Collaborative Care Model. Is a member of the Collaborative Care team in the Student Wellness Hub for the management of students with ongoing (mental) health concerns. Participates in Student Wellness Hub & Student Services meetings & trainings. The Wellness Access Advisor may spend some of their time facilitating workshops or conducting outreach presentations. Performs other related duties, as needed. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Master’s degree in Social Work, Counselling, or any other relevant field with licensure to perform psychosocial interventions, strongly preferred. Member in good standing of their professional order with license to practice in Quebec. Two years of relevant clinical experience in a mental health setting involving crisis situations. Experience in an emergency setting, a strong asset. Knowledge of private and public mental health resources in the Quebec context. Ability to intervene in crisis situations, Suicide Prevention Centre of Montreal (Centre de Prévention du Suicide de Montréal) Training a strong asset. Ability to make professionally autonomous decisions in coordinating patient care in a decentralized model. Analytical and problem-solving skills, professional autonomy, flexibility, creativity and tolerance to stress. Experience with youth and the University setting would be considered an asset. Expertise working with one or more specific student populations including international students, Asian and Pacific Islander students, Indigenous students, graduate students or other marginalized student populations, an asset. Ability to navigate complex student situations, including crisis intervention. Proven ability to communicate, both one-on-one and in a group setting, with tact, diplomacy and sensitivity in a cross-cultural environment to a diverse population. Ability to support students from diverse communities drawing upon a combination of personal lived experience, formal training and previous work experience is a strong asset.  Demonstrated skills in a PC environment using MS Word, Excel, PowerPoint, e-mail and internet search engines. Proficiency in Mandarin also a strong asset.    Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day-to-day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (fluent) on a scale of 0-4 As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2B - Student Services - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director Wellness (Communities) Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Invigilators----Metabolic-Biochemistry---BIOC-311---Fall-2026_JR0000080712": {
      "title": "Invigilators -  Metabolic Biochemistry - BIOC 311 - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biochemistry Course Title and Course Number: Metabolic Biochemistry - BIOC 311 Estimated Number of Positions: 4 invigilators on October 7, 2026 - Two invigilators per exam room. 6 invigilators on November 19, 2026 - Two invigilators per exam room. Number of Hours of per position: 3 hours Dates & location of the Mid-Term Exam: Wednesday, October 07, 2026 - 3 hours (7.00 pm – 10:00 pm) McIntyre 504 McIntyre 522 Thursday, November 19, 2026 - 3 hours (7.00 pm – 10:00 pm) McIntyre 521 McIntyre 522 McIntyre 1034 Position Summary: Invigilators will be responsible for Invigilating BIOC 311 mid term exams. IMPORTANT: Applications will not be considered in situations of potential personal or academic conflict of interest. It is the responsibility of the applicant to report any potential personal or academic conflict of interest to the Department of Biochemistry. Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. Assist the instructor in organizing and sorting the exam booklets after the examination has concluded. Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. Handles students’ problems. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Responsible to report any behavior by students or others that does not comply with University regulations.  Ensures that all mobile and web-accessible electronic devices are collected from the students prior to the start of the examination and returned upon their departure. Ensures that students are accompanied to the washroom. Alerts professor when students have a question.  At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensuring delivery of the exams. Other qualifying skills & abilities: Must be client-focused and service oriented. Punctual. Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately.  Demonstrated ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems.  Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team.  Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams.  English, spoken and read.  French an asset. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-27 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Leacock-Building/Invigilator_JR0000080390": {
      "title": "Invigilator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biology Exam Period: BIOL219 Tuesday, October 6, 2026 6:30 - 9 pm (LEA 232 and LEA 26). BIOL300 Wednesday, October 7, 2026 7:30-9:00 pm (LEA219). Please also take into account setup, takedown and training time, which will be added. Position Summary: Responsible for setting up the examination rooms for mid-term exams according to specific requirements. Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Alerts professor when students have a question or a concern, if arises. At the end of the examination, period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensure delivery of the exams. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Understanding and commitment to maintaining appropriate standards of confidentiality and security of materials. Adhering to McGill University policies and procedures. Ability to work independently; Dependability, reliability, punctuality. Education/Experience: Invigilating experience an asset. Must be enrolled as a Graduate Student, McGill University. An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their Supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Invigilator----Protein-Structure-and-Function---BIOC-450---Fall-2026_JR0000080710": {
      "title": "Invigilator -  Protein Structure and Function - BIOC 450 - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biochemistry Course Title and Course Number: Protein Structure and Function - BIOC 450 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 (One) Position Total Hours of Work per Term: 3.5 hours Date of the mid-term examination: Monday, September 28, 2026 - 3.5 hours (6:00pm – 9:30pm) Position Summary: Invigilator will be responsible for Invigilating BIOC 450 mid term exams. IMPORTANT: Applications will not be considered in situations of potential personal or academic conflict of interest. It is the responsibility of the applicant to report any potential personal or academic conflict of interest to the Department of Biochemistry. Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly.   Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. Handles students’ problems. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Responsible to report any behavior by students or others that does not comply with University regulations.  Ensures that all mobile and web-accessible electronic devices are collected from the students prior to the start of the examination and returned upon their departure. Ensures that students are accompanied to the washroom. Alerts professor when students have a question.  At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensuring delivery of the exams. Other qualifying skills & abilities: Must be client-focused and service oriented. Punctual. Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately.  Demonstrated ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems.  Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team.  Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams.  English, spoken and read.  French an asset. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-Music/Office-Assistant-C--Graduate-Studies---Schulich-_JR0000080709": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C (Graduate Studies - Schulich)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Student Services - Schulich School of Music Position Summary: Under the direction of the Manager of Student Services, Schulich School of Music, the incumbent may perform some or all of the listed general administration functions. These functions are of high complexity, and the incumbent performs them using general instruction. Key Responsibilities: Front counter coverage, respond to general enquiries Assist in organizing and scheduling Comprehensive Exams, general exams and Exam Recitals for graduate students Follow up with examiners regarding assessment and evaluation Collects data and prepare entries for payroll Prepares letters for approval (could be for scholarships, immigration papers, etc).  Assists with the follow-up of missing documents from students.   Email correspondence, respond to enquiries, follows up as needed Direct graduate students to appropriate resources Prepare files for archives Other duties related to Graduate Studies Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Dynamic and enthusiastic with strong interpersonal skills. Good communication in person and via email. Demonstrated organizational skills and strong attention to detail. Enjoys working independently and as part of a team. In-depth knowledge of music and instrumental/vocal ensembles. Knowledge of the McGill University, Schulich School of Music graduate programs is an asset. Knowledge of Microsoft Word, Excel. Basic French is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $17.17 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 15 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-01 Position End Date: 2026-11-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-23 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/TA--Fall-2026--Applied-Health-and-Physical-Assessment-Anatomy-1-NUR1-338-001_JR0000080711": {
      "title": "TA  Fall 2026: Applied Health and Physical Assessment/Anatomy 1-NUR1 338 001",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Ingram School of Nursing Course Title: Applied Health and Physical Assessment/Anatomy Course Code: NUR1 338 005 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Hiring Unit: Ingram School of Nursing Course Title and Course Number: Applied Health and Physical Assessment/Anatomy 1- NUR1 338 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 hours per position Dates: September 21, 2026 to December 22, 2026 Job Description: Under the supervision of the course coordinator, the Teaching Assistant (TA) supports the delivery of the Health and Physical Assessment course for nursing students. This includes reviewing course content, assisting with student learning, participating in evaluation activities, participating in anatomy labs and helping implement the course plan. TAs are available to answer student questions, may assist during office hours or review sessions, and contribute to a positive and supportive learning environment. Mix of synchronous and asynchronous work. Salary: $38.46/hour Desired Major(s): Nursing Desired Degree Level:   Masters & Grad Dips & Certs Qualifications: Applicants must have successfully completed Health and Physical Assessment courses (NUR1 338/339 or NUR1 235/236 or NUR2 534/535). Masters or Doctoral student at McGill. Prior experience as a Teaching Assistant for NUR1 338, NUR1 339, or NUR1 235, NUR1 236 or NUR2 534, NUR2 535 is preferred. Graduate teaching experience is an asset. Bilingualism in French and English is an asset. Must be legally eligible to work in Canada. McGill University is Committed to Equity in Employment. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-09-21 Position End Date: 2026-12-22 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    }
